<BaseLayout title="About & Editorial Method" description="How Dama Korea researches, writes, illustrates, reviews and updates its independent Korean heritage field guides." activeNav="/about/">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="shell visual-shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">About Dama Korea</p><h1>Independent field guides, with the method left visible.</h1><p class="page-hero__lede">Dama Korea publishes English-language guidance for understanding and visiting Korean heritage. It is an independent publishing project based in South Korea.</p></div></div></section>
  <article class="legal-shell article-body">
    <h2>Who publishes the site</h2><p>Dama Korea is operated under the Dama Korea publishing name. It is not a government body, heritage authority, museum, religious organization or travel agency. Articles are attributed to the Dama Korea Editorial Desk unless a real named contributor agrees to a byline.</p><p>We do not invent a traveler, specialist biography, interview or first-person visit to make a guide sound more authoritative.</p>
    <h2>What makes a field guide useful</h2><p>Official pages can confirm an opening notice or define a monument, but a visitor still needs to choose a route, recognize what matters and prepare for points of failure. Our original value comes from that synthesis: comparisons, spatial explanations, annotated sequences and reusable decision tools.</p>
    <h2>Research and source roles</h2><p>Each guide begins with the organization responsible for the site or rule. Historical interpretation is checked against appropriate heritage institutions, museums and specialist sources. Time-sensitive statements link to the page that readers should recheck, and each source is labeled with the claim or decision it supports.</p>
    <h2>Images and illustrations</h2><p>Every published visual must have recorded rights, credit and descriptive text. Explanatory illustrations are labeled as illustrations and are never presented as documentary evidence of a visit or current site condition.</p>
    <h2>Publication gate</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>The guide solves one defined visitor or interpretation problem.</li><li>Historical and time-sensitive claims use sources appropriate to their role.</li><li>The page adds an original route, comparison, diagram, observation framework or decision tool.</li><li>Image rights, accessibility text and captions are complete.</li><li>Dates and material changes are recorded honestly.</li><li>Draft, empty and unverified pages remain outside the public build.</li></ul>
    <h2>Corrections and independence</h2><p>Readers can use the <a href="/contact/">public corrections channel</a> to identify a changed source or unclear passage. Future advertising or disclosed affiliate relationships will not purchase favorable editorial conclusions.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
